x86_64-linux  every difference is a static local or its guard, the divergence docs/CONFORMANCE.md records under "A static local keeps its own name in the object file": cxx1 spells one `<function's mangled symbol>.name` - `_ZN1S1fEv.n`, `_ZZ4mainENK3$_0clEi.n`, `main.n`, `_ZN1S1gEv.l` with `_ZN1S1gEv.l.guard` - where clang writes `_ZZN1S1fEvE1n`, `_ZZZ4mainENK3$_0clEiE1n`, `_ZZ4mainE1n`, `_ZZN1S1gEvE1l` with `_ZGVZN1S1gEvE1l`; the C1/D1 forms of the in-class constructors and destructors, the rule mem-init-two-rules.nonames records; and `__cxa_guard_abort`, which clang calls from the unwind path if the constructor throws while the guard is held, a path cxx1 does not emit yet (static-local-constructor.nonames says the same). Nothing links against a static local, and what this case measures - that every one of them is a distinct object - is what run.sh checks.
arm64-darwin  the same, with Mach-O's leading underscore.
x86_64-windows  the same static locals against cl's `?n@?1??f@S@@QEAAHXZ@4HA` form and its `?$TSS0@` guard, `_Init_thread_abort` for the unwind path cxx1 does not emit, and the closure type, `$_0` against `<lambda_0>`, which lookup-order.nonames records.
